  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/74/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_122.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Strange returns home, exhausted from his \"unending battle against the supernatural forces which menace mankind!\"&nbsp; He finally succumbs to sleep, but when he opens his eyes, he sees \"a mysterious figure cloaked in darkness... covered with an aura of evil!\"&nbsp; He finds his powers useless!&nbsp; (My guess is that he hasn't actually woken up -- and sure enough, only a few panels later, I discover that I am <i>correct</i>!)&nbsp; \"I am caught in the Nightmare World!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=(Horrors!)]\n\nTurns out he \"forgot to utter the protective chant which keeps me safe from harm when I shut my eyes in repose!\"&nbsp; His enemy Nightmare has been waiting for a mistake like this!&nbsp; And Strange is finally captured in a sphere and deposited before Nightmare himself.&nbsp; He proceeds to give Strange a preview of all the horrible things he can do to him, because apparently, \"only <i>you</i> have defeated me in the past!\"\n\nStrange is bound in irons, but apparently Nightmare's focus on Strange has allowed \"the Gulgol\" to appear, Nightmare's \"mortal enemy from the netherworld!\"&nbsp; Nightmare can never defeat him because he never sleeps!&nbsp; Nightmare has no choice but to free Strange and ask for his help.&nbsp; Strange is easily able to turn the Gulgol away, leaving Nightmare incredulous.\n\nBut it turns out that Dr. Strange had only <i>hypnotized</i> Nightmare into thinking the Gulgol was there, meaning that he used only his <i>brain</i> to escape!&nbsp; To which Nightmare can only cry, \"Curse you!!&nbsp; You outsmarted me!!\"&nbsp; Strange then makes his escape with Nightmare left only to call out, \"I'll get you yet, accursed one!&nbsp; I can wait for all <i>eternity</i> if need be!\"\n\nStrange then awakens and is safe once more.&nbsp; As he stares out at the dawn over the city, the editorial blurb reads:\n\n<i>Hark to these words, loyal reader -- one day, many years hence, you will proudly relate these <b>Dr. Strange</b> tales to a new generation -- a generation which will envy the fact that <b>you</b> were privileged to have read the earliest of these trend-setting tales!&nbsp; More next issue!</i>",
  "grade_one_liner": "A realm of nightmares, arbitrary magic, and endless fist-shaking\nmakes sleep look increasingly appealing.&nbsp; But... <b>dare we</b>?",
  "subtitle": "Now Accepting Caffeine Donations",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | SoTired | width=15%] [ASSET: right | StillAsleep | width=15%] It didn't take a rocket scientist to work out what had happened in the opening panels, after Strange fell asleep on his desk.\n[BR]\n[BR] As you perhaps read in my Plot Summary, I guessed, even before Dr. Strange did, that the explanation for his inexplicable loss of powers was probably simple... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he was still asleep!]\n\nYet, far from being satisfied by my [SPAN: severity=success; text=brilliant deduction], Strange being stuck in the \"nightmare world\" could only mean that readers would soon be subjugated to the villainous [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Nightmare] himself... <i>again</i>!&nbsp; (And even though we already knew this from the splash page, I found myself overcome by feelings of <i>dread</i>...)\n\n[ASSET: left | GoofyNightmare | width=18%] Nightmare looked just plain silly... and <i>hardly</i> menacing.\n[BR]\n[BR] Strange's use of hypnosis to fool him into freeing him felt like a total cop-out.\n[BR] Worse, it means that the entire threat evaporates the moment Strange decides to be clever.\n[BR]\n[BR] And Nightmare's dialog when they first stood face-to-face, and then <i>particularly</i> at the end, was right out of \"the late late show\" -- in other words, [SPAN: severity=error; text=absolutely barf-worthy.]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Doesn't this guy have better things to do?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(He has an entire <i>realm</i> to rule over!&nbsp; Can't he just be content with <i>that</i>?&nbsp; Even <i>villains</i> need motives, and this idiot seems to have <i>none</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nDon't you find it interesting that Strange was <i>soooo</i> tired at the beginning of this adventure?\n\nSince he has no actual life (at least none that any reader has ever been made privy to), [SPAN: severity=warning; text=how can he be so exhausted?]&nbsp; It's not like the previous handful of adventures have been <i>that</i> taxing.&nbsp; After all, he was up for an evening stroll around the block as recently as [LINK:1964:ST_120_strange2|ST #120strange|2027-06-10]!\n\n<i>Sure, </i>there was the matter of finding his missing body last month, which <i>did</i> mean he had to stay awake for an entire 24 hours while out hunting for it, so... <i>okay</i> then... [SPAN: severity=info; text=<i>maybe</i> he's earned a nap after all!]&nbsp; But I think it is <i>much </i>more likely that he was \"exhausted\" simply because the plot needed him to sleep.&nbsp; (At least, that's the way <b><i>I</i></b> see it...)\n\nBut still, anyone <i>that</i> tired would <i>surely</i> have at least walked back to his bedroom and put on his pajamas!&nbsp; <i>Perhaps brushed his <b>teeth</b> at least...?</i>  [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=<i>Anything</i> other than simply lying his head down on his desk and crashing...!]\n\nAnd then to find that he needed to \"utter the protective chant which keeps me safe from harm\" any time he so much as closes his eyes, or risk being captured by Nightmare!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]\n\nStrange has <i>enough</i> problems on Earth, where Baron Bored-o is constantly trying to bump him off -- and the guy can't even fall asleep?&nbsp; Talk about <i>pressure!</i>\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I think you can see none of this worked for me.]\n\nIf the goal was to elicit empathy or concern over the well-being of this character, the co-creators [SPAN: severity=error; text=failed miserably.]&nbsp; Problems like these are [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=far removed] from the down-to-earth, more mundane (and frankly, more <i>relatable</i>) problems of Peter Parker.\n\n[ASSET: right | BoundInChains | width=21%] Instead, in this \"make-it-up-as-you-go\" mode of storytelling, Dr. Strange is:\n[HANG] * encased in a transparent sphere\n[HANG] * shrunk to palm size\n[HANG] * turned to stone\n[HANG] * banished to the \"world of nothingness\"\n[HANG] * dropped into a bottomless pit\n[HANG] * bound in iron chains\n[HANG] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And that's only a couple of pages worth!&nbsp; <b><i>Ouch!</i></b>)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | AttackOfTheGulgol | width=12%] [ASSET: right | YouDidIt | width=12%] Only the sudden arrival of the Gulgol distracts Nightmare from his planned torment of Dr. Strange.\n[BR]\n[BR] (This was made necessary since there were only about two-and-a-half pages left...)\n\nForced into restoring his arch-enemy's powers to deal with the sudden threat, Nightmare capitulates and watches in horror as Dr. Strange does nothing more than snap his fingers, and the Gulgol is gone.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Problem solved!]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=But it was all a <i>trick</i>!]\n\n[HANG0] Strange gets away, leaving Nightmare to quote once more from the [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Super-Villain Playbook] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(patent pending)]:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=a fist-shaking shout of, \"I'll get you <i>next </i>time!\"]\n[ASSET: center | CurseYouStrange | width=large]\n\n<i>Granted</i>, it isn't a word-for-word repeat of the climax of Nightmare's <i>last</i> attempt to triumph over Dr. Strange that we saw only six months ago (back in [LINK:1963:ST_116_strange2|ST #116strange|2027-02-22]), but the refrain is unmistakable.\n\nAnd if that weren't bad enough, the <i>ultimate</i> insult comes in the final panel where the editorial blurb talks about being able to tell your grandchildren that you were \"privileged\" to have read this early Dr. Strange adventure!\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=I beg to differ!]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=Dreck such as this comes across as exactly the <i>opposite</i> -- <b>as a warning!</b>]\n\n[HANG0] To any shepherd interested in herding a new generation toward Marvel:\n[BR] this tale is a danger I would <i>shield</i> him or her from!\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Words... fail... me....]\n\nThis was, without a <i>doubt</i>, [SPAN: severity=error; text=one of the <i>worst</i> Doctor Strange stories of all time!]\n\nIt is the poster child of \"let's make this up as we go along\" -- which the subject matter <i>unfortunately</i> lends itself heavily toward (and is something the co-creators still think they can get away with).\n\nI was very tempted to give this a FR 1.0 rating, but I figured that at <i>least</i> the follow-up to the previous Nightmare appearance from issue 116 was worth a half point.&nbsp; That and the notion that a character such as Nightmare would naturally have an enemy who did not need sleep as his presumably unbeatable foe (which at least makes sense).&nbsp; Ultimately, these two things rescued the story from getting bottom marks.\n\nNightmare keeps saying that only Dr. Strange has ever beaten him, but readers still have been told <i>nothing</i> about that encounter.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Isn't it time for an actual explanation?]\n\nThe Dr. Strange half of <i>Strange Tales</i> is fast becoming [SPAN: severity=warning; text=the story I absolutely dread reading every month.]&nbsp; The character itself has the potential to show promise, but these stories do nothing to showcase that potential, and quite frankly, they threaten to put me to sleep every time I sit down with them!\n\nAnd now, after reading this, I have to fear even <i>that</i>!\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Brutal!&nbsp; <b>Absolutely</b> brutal!]\n\nPlus, it doesn't help that this is the [SPAN: severity=error; text=third issue in a row] to end with Dr. Strange facing away from the \"camera,\" either walking into the distance or standing... contemplating...\n\n[HANG0] Not only does it do nothing other than to help cement my feelings of deja vu,\n[BR] but the promise of \"<i>More next issue!</i>\" now feels like a <b>threat</b>!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Why am I doing this to myself?]",
  "highlight": "A \"Nightmare\" of a villain wanted Dr. Strange asleep, but it was the comic that nearly got its wish."
}